      <section id="welcome" class="handbook-introduction">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Welcome to River Guides</p>
          <h2>Today we are going to make one complicated topic simple.</h2>

          <p class="lead">
            Buying a home is one of the biggest financial decisions most people will ever make.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many families, it is more than an investment. It is where birthdays are celebrated,
            holidays are spent, children grow up, and memories are made. It represents years of
            work, sacrifice, and planning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Yet one of the most important parts of protecting that investment is also one of the
            least understood: homeowners insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people do not buy homeowners insurance because they are excited to learn about
            deductibles, liability limits, or replacement costs. They buy it because they are
            purchasing a house, their mortgage company requires it, or they are looking for a
            better price than they are paying today.
          </p>

          <p>Then life gets busy. The policy gets filed away. Hopefully, it is never needed.</p>

          <p>
            Unfortunately, the first time many homeowners truly look at their policy is after
            something has already gone wrong.
          </p>

          <div class="question-stack" aria-label="Common questions after a loss">
            <p>What does my policy actually cover?</p>
            <p>Why do I have to pay a deductible?</p>
            <p>Why did insurance not cover all of it?</p>
            <p>Why did my premium increase this year?</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are good questions. The problem is that they are usually asked after the loss
            has already happened.
          </p>

          <p>
            This handbook is designed to answer them before that day ever comes.
          </p>

          <p>
            My goal is not to convince you to buy insurance from a particular company. It is not
            to tell you that one carrier is always better than another. It is not even to convince
            you to buy the most expensive policy available.
          </p>

          <p>My goal is much simpler: I want you to understand what you are buying.</p>

          <p>
            Once you understand how homeowners insurance works, you can begin making decisions
            based on knowledge instead of guesswork.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether you eventually purchase a policy through me, another agent, or directly from
            an insurance company, I hope you leave this guide understanding more than when you began.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-1">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part I</p>
          <h2>Understanding Homeowners Insurance</h2>
          <blockquote>
            Insurance is not about removing every risk. It is about deciding which risks
            you are willing to keep and which ones you want help carrying.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-1" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 1</p>
          <h2>What Homeowners Insurance Actually Is</h2>

          <p>
            If you asked ten homeowners what their insurance policy protects, you would probably
            hear ten different answers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people would tell you it protects the mortgage. Others would say it protects the
            value of the home. Some simply believe it is something the bank forces them to buy.
          </p>

          <p>Each answer contains a piece of the truth, but none of them tells the whole story.</p>

          <p>
            Homeowners insurance exists to help protect you financially after a
            <strong>covered loss</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those two words are some of the most important words in this entire handbook.
          </p>

          <p>
            Insurance does not promise to pay for everything that can happen to your home.
            It does not prevent disasters, stop storms, keep pipes from freezing, or prevent theft.
            What it does is help reduce the financial burden when certain covered events occur
            according to the terms of your policy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Understanding what your policy covers is just as important as understanding what it does not.
          </p>

          <h3>It does not protect your mortgage balance</h3>

          <p>
            One of the most common misconceptions is that a home should be insured for whatever
            the owner paid for it or whatever remains on the mortgage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homeowners insurance is generally asking a different question:
          </p>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            If this home had to be rebuilt after a covered loss, what would it cost to rebuild it today?
          </div>

          <p>
            That is why two homes that sell for the same price may need very different amounts of insurance.
            The land, neighborhood, school district, and housing market influence market value.
            Insurance is focused primarily on rebuilding the structure.
          </p>

          <h3>It is a tool for managing risk</h3>

          <p>
            Every family has different priorities. Every home carries different risks. Every budget is different.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some homeowners want the broadest protection they can reasonably afford. Others accept a
            higher deductible in exchange for a lower premium. Some own valuable jewelry, collections,
            workshops, detached garages, or additional property. Others do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is not one perfect homeowners policy. There are policies that fit different situations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Helping someone with insurance should not mean automatically selling the most expensive policy.
            It should mean explaining the trade-offs well enough for the homeowner to make an informed decision.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“The policy protects whatever my home is worth.”</h3>
            <p>
              Your purchase price, tax appraisal, market value, mortgage balance, and estimated rebuilding
              cost may all be different numbers.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Homeowners insurance is designed to help protect you financially after covered losses.
              It is not based solely on your mortgage or your home's market value.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-2" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 2</p>
          <h2>Price Matters—But It Is Not the Whole Story</h2>

          <p>
            One of the first things many new insurance agents are taught is that insurance is not about price.
          </p>

          <p>I understand why people say that. But for most families, price absolutely matters.</p>

          <p>
            Insurance competes with groceries, mortgage payments, car payments, childcare, utilities,
            and every other demand on a household budget. Pretending price does not matter does not help anyone.
          </p>

          <p>At the same time, price is not the only thing that matters.</p>

          <p>
            A lower premium usually comes with a trade-off somewhere. It may mean a higher deductible,
            less personal property protection, Actual Cash Value instead of Replacement Cost,
            or fewer optional coverages.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of those choices is automatically right or wrong. They are decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is not to spend the most money or the least money. The goal is to understand
            exactly what you are paying for—and what you are choosing not to pay for.
          </p>

          <p>
            A homeowner with substantial savings may comfortably choose a higher deductible.
            A homeowner living paycheck to paycheck may prefer a lower deductible, even if it costs
            more each month. Neither choice is automatically correct.
          </p>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              The best policy is not automatically the cheapest or the most expensive. It is the policy
              that balances meaningful protection with a price that makes sense for your life.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part I</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Homeowners insurance responds only to covered losses.</li>
              <li>Rebuilding cost is different from market value and mortgage balance.</li>
              <li>Every coverage decision is a decision about how much risk you keep.</li>
              <li>Price matters, but it must be compared alongside coverage and deductibles.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-2">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part II</p>
          <h2>Protecting Your Home</h2>
          <blockquote>
            A house is more than walls and a roof. It is where life happens.
            Homeowners insurance begins by protecting the structure that protects everything else.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-3" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 3</p>
          <h2>Dwelling Coverage: Protecting the Home Itself</h2>

          <p>
            Imagine lightning strikes your home at two o'clock in the morning. Within minutes,
            a fire spreads through the attic. The fire department arrives quickly, but by sunrise,
            much of the home has been destroyed.
          </p>

          <p>This is where <strong>Dwelling Coverage</strong> comes in.</p>

          <p>
            Dwelling Coverage is the foundation of most homeowners policies. If a covered loss damages
            or destroys the structure itself, this coverage helps repair or rebuild it, subject to the
            policy terms and limits.
          </p>

          <p>It commonly applies to permanently attached parts of the home, including:</p>

          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>The roof and exterior walls</li>
            <li>Interior walls and floors</li>
            <li>Built-in cabinets and fixtures</li>
            <li>Plumbing and electrical systems</li>
            <li>Heating and air-conditioning systems</li>
            <li>Attached garages</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Rebuilding estimates may consider square footage, construction type, roof materials,
            interior finishes, labor costs, material costs, and special features.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“My home is insured for what it is worth.”</h3>
            <p>
              Dwelling Coverage is generally based on estimated rebuilding cost, not solely on market value.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Dwelling Coverage protects the structure of your home and is generally built around
              the estimated cost to rebuild after a covered loss.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-4" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 4</p>
          <h2>How the Coverages Connect</h2>

          <p>
            One of the easiest ways to understand a homeowners policy is to realize that many coverages
            are connected rather than selected completely independently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think of Dwelling Coverage as the trunk of a tree. Other Structures, Personal Property,
            Loss of Use, and certain optional coverages may be calculated partly as percentages of it,
            depending on the insurer and policy form.
          </p>

          <div class="coverage-tree" role="img" aria-label="Dwelling coverage connects to other structures, personal property, loss of use, building codes, and water backup coverage">
            <div class="coverage-tree__root">Dwelling Coverage</div>
            <div class="coverage-tree__branches">
              <span>Other Structures</span>
              <span>Personal Property</span>
              <span>Loss of Use</span>
              <span>Building Codes*</span>
              <span>Water Backup*</span>
            </div>
            <p>*When selected or included, depending on the policy.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Once you understand this relationship, the declarations page becomes easier to read.
            Instead of seeing a collection of unrelated numbers, you can see how the policy is built.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-5" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 5</p>
          <h2>Other Structures</h2>

          <p>
            A windstorm may leave the main house untouched while destroying a detached garage,
            shed, workshop, fence, or gazebo.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other Structures Coverage is designed to help protect structures on the property that
            are separated from the main dwelling, subject to the policy terms.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own multiple detached buildings or unusually valuable structures, ask whether
            the standard amount is appropriate.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“Everything on my property is covered the same way as the house.”</h3>
            <p>
              Attached and detached structures may fall under different coverage categories and limits.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Detached structures often have their own coverage limit. Make sure your policy reflects
              what is actually on your property today.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-6" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 6</p>
          <h2>Building Codes Coverage: The Upgrade You Never Planned For</h2>

          <p>
            Imagine your home was built in 1975 and a covered fire damages a large portion of it.
            The contractor explains that the damaged area cannot simply be rebuilt the way it was.
            Current electrical, framing, roof decking, or mechanical requirements may be different.
          </p>

          <p>
            Building Codes Coverage—often called Ordinance or Law Coverage—can help with increased
            rebuilding costs caused by updated laws or building requirements after a covered loss.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older homes may benefit from this coverage most because construction standards can change
            significantly over several decades.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“Insurance automatically pays for every required code upgrade.”</h3>
            <p>
              Coverage for increased costs caused by updated codes depends on the policy and applicable limits.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Building Codes Coverage helps address the difference between repairing yesterday's house
              and rebuilding according to today's requirements.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part II</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Dwelling Coverage is the structural foundation of the policy.</li>
              <li>Several other coverages may be connected to the dwelling amount.</li>
              <li>Detached structures may need separate attention.</li>
              <li>Older homes may face added rebuilding costs because of updated codes.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-3">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part III</p>
          <h2>Protecting Everything You Own</h2>
          <blockquote>
            A house can be rebuilt. The memories inside it cannot.
            Insurance cannot replace sentimental value, but it can help replace many of the things
            that make a house feel like home.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-7" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 7</p>
          <h2>Personal Property: Everything That Makes Your House a Home</h2>

          <p>
            Imagine picking up your house, turning it upside down, and shaking everything out.
            What falls out is a useful way to think about Personal Property Coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your furniture, clothing, televisions, dishes, tools, children's toys, computers,
            appliances, decorations, and other movable belongings may fall into this category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people underestimate how much it would cost to replace everything they own at the same time.
            One couch, one mattress, or one television may not seem overwhelming. Replacing an entire
            household after a major fire can add up quickly.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“Everything inside my house is automatically covered for whatever it is worth.”</h3>
            <p>
              Personal Property Coverage has limits, conditions, and sometimes category-specific sublimits.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Personal Property Coverage helps protect the belongings you have spent years accumulating,
              up to the limits and conditions of your policy.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-8" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 8</p>
          <h2>Replacement Cost vs. Actual Cash Value</h2>

          <p>
            Suppose a six-year-old television is destroyed in a covered fire. A comparable new television
            costs about $1,200. How much should the insurance company pay?
          </p>

          <p>The answer depends on how the property is valued.</p>

          <h3>Replacement Cost Value</h3>
          <p>
            Replacement Cost Value generally focuses on what it costs to replace damaged property with
            comparable new property, subject to policy terms and claim requirements.
          </p>

          <h3>Actual Cash Value</h3>
          <p>
            Actual Cash Value generally considers depreciation. The claim payment may reflect the item's
            age and condition rather than the full cost of a new replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement Cost usually provides broader protection. Actual Cash Value may reduce the premium.
            The choice is a trade-off, not a moral judgment.
          </p>

          <div class="agent-questions">
            <p class="card-label">Question to ask your agent</p>
            <p>Is my personal property covered on a Replacement Cost or Actual Cash Value basis?</p>
          </div>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Replacement Cost and Actual Cash Value determine how damaged property may be valued after
              a covered loss. Know which one applies before you need it.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-9" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 9</p>
          <h2>Valuable Items: When Standard Coverage Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            A policy may provide a large overall personal property limit while still placing lower limits
            on certain categories, such as jewelry, firearms, musical instruments, artwork, collectibles,
            coins, or other high-value property.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scheduled Personal Property or Valuable Items Coverage may allow specific items to be listed
            and insured separately. Documentation may include appraisals, receipts, photographs, or
            certificates of authenticity.
          </p>

          <p>
            The time to discover a special limit is before an item is lost or stolen—not after.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“My overall personal property limit guarantees full coverage for every expensive item.”</h3>
            <p>
              Certain categories may have lower limits unless additional coverage is selected.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="agent-questions">
            <p class="card-label">Questions to ask your agent</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Are there special limits for jewelry, firearms, collectibles, art, or instruments?</li>
              <li>Should any items be scheduled separately?</li>
              <li>What documentation should I keep?</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part III</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Replacing an entire household can cost far more than expected.</li>
              <li>Replacement Cost and Actual Cash Value are materially different.</li>
              <li>High-value items may need separate coverage and documentation.</li>
              <li>A simple home inventory can make a future claim much easier.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-4">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part IV</p>
          <h2>Protecting Yourself</h2>
          <blockquote>
            Not every loss involves the house. Sometimes the greatest financial risk is what happens
            when someone says you were responsible.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-10" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 10</p>
          <h2>Family Liability Protection</h2>

          <p>
            Personal Liability Coverage helps protect your financial future when you are legally responsible
            for covered bodily injury or property damage to someone else.
          </p>

          <p>
            A child may accidentally damage a neighbor's property. A dog may bite a visitor.
            A guest may be injured because of a dangerous condition. Depending on the facts and policy,
            liability coverage may help with covered damages and legal defense costs.
          </p>

          <h3>How much liability coverage should you carry?</h3>

          <p>There is not one number that is right for everyone.</p>

          <p>
            For many homeowners, I commonly recommend <strong>$300,000</strong> in Personal Liability Coverage.
            In my experience, it provides a meaningful level of protection for many families while remaining affordable.
          </p>

          <p>
            If someone owns multiple homes, rental properties, significant investments, or other substantial assets,
            that is when it may make sense to discuss $500,000, $1 million, or an umbrella policy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is not to choose the highest limit available. It is to choose a level of protection
            that matches the homeowner's financial situation and the assets they have worked to build.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“Liability only matters when someone is injured inside my home.”</h3>
            <p>
              Coverage may extend to other situations where you are legally responsible, depending on the policy and facts.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Liability coverage helps protect your financial future—not just the physical house.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-11" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 11</p>
          <h2>Guest Medical Coverage</h2>

          <p>
            Guest Medical Coverage, often called Medical Payments to Others, may help pay certain medical
            expenses when a guest is injured, regardless of fault, subject to the policy terms and limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is generally intended for relatively minor incidents and is not a replacement for health insurance.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“Guest Medical Coverage and liability are the same thing.”</h3>
            <p>
              They are related but serve different purposes and usually carry very different limits.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-12" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 12</p>
          <h2>Loss of Use: When Home No Longer Feels Like Home</h2>

          <p>
            A covered loss may leave the house standing but temporarily unsafe to live in.
            Loss of Use Coverage, often called Additional Living Expense, helps with certain
            additional costs caused by that displacement.
          </p>

          <p>Depending on the policy and circumstances, those costs may include:</p>

          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Hotel stays or temporary housing</li>
            <li>Additional meal expenses when cooking at home is not possible</li>
            <li>Other reasonable increases in living costs caused by the covered loss</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The key word is <strong>additional</strong>. The coverage is generally intended to address
            the increase above normal living expenses, not every expense a family already had.
          </p>

          <div class="agent-questions">
            <p class="card-label">Question to ask your agent</p>
            <p>If my family could not live here for six months, how would this coverage help us?</p>
          </div>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              A covered loss can disrupt an entire life, not just damage a structure.
              Loss of Use helps families continue living while repairs are completed.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part IV</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Liability protects against certain legal responsibilities to others.</li>
              <li>$300,000 is a common recommendation for many families, while greater assets may justify more.</li>
              <li>Guest Medical Coverage can address certain smaller injuries.</li>
              <li>Loss of Use helps with additional costs when a covered loss makes the home uninhabitable.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-5">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part V</p>
          <h2>Choosing Your Policy</h2>
          <blockquote>
            The right policy is built one decision at a time.
            Every coverage you choose—and every coverage you decline—shapes how it may respond later.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-13" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 13</p>
          <h2>Understanding Deductibles</h2>

          <p>
            A deductible is the amount you agree to absorb toward a covered loss before the insurer pays
            the remaining covered amount, subject to the policy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A higher deductible often lowers the premium because you are keeping more of the risk.
            A lower deductible usually costs more because the insurance company takes on more of it.
          </p>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            If something happened tomorrow, could you comfortably pay this deductible without creating a second emergency?
          </div>

          <h3>Dollar deductibles vs. percentage deductibles</h3>

          <p>
            Wind and hail deductibles are often expressed as percentages. A 1% deductible typically means
            1% of the Dwelling Coverage—not 1% of the repair bill.
          </p>

          <div class="learning-example">
            <p class="card-label">Learning example</p>
            <p>Dwelling Coverage: <strong>$400,000</strong></p>
            <p>1% wind and hail deductible: <strong>$4,000</strong></p>
            <p>2% wind and hail deductible: <strong>$8,000</strong></p>
          </div>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“A 1% deductible means I pay 1% of the repair cost.”</h3>
            <p>
              Percentage deductibles are commonly based on the insured dwelling amount.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              A deductible is not just a discount lever. It is a financial commitment you may need to meet tomorrow.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-14" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 14</p>
          <h2>Why Insurance Costs Change</h2>

          <p>
            Homeowners often ask why premiums rise even when they have not filed a claim.
            The answer is that insurance pricing reflects more than one household's individual history.
          </p>

          <p>Rates may be influenced by factors such as:</p>

          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Construction material and labor costs</li>
            <li>Inflation</li>
            <li>Local and regional claim trends</li>
            <li>Severe weather and catastrophe exposure</li>
            <li>Reinsurance costs</li>
            <li>Property characteristics</li>
            <li>Individual claims history, where applicable</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Insurance is built on pooled risk. When the overall cost of rebuilding homes and paying claims rises,
            premiums can rise even for homeowners who have not personally filed a claim.
          </p>

          <aside class="misconception-card">
            <p class="card-label">Common misconception</p>
            <h3>“No claims means my premium should never increase.”</h3>
            <p>
              Personal claims history is only one of many factors that may influence pricing.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-15" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 15</p>
          <h2>Discounts: Saving Money the Right Way</h2>

          <p>
            Discounts can improve value, but a lower premium is useful only if the policy still provides
            the protection you need.
          </p>

          <p>Common discounts may include:</p>

          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Bundling home and auto insurance</li>
            <li>Qualifying security or fire-protection systems</li>
            <li>Impact-resistant roofing, where available</li>
            <li>Newer home construction</li>
            <li>Claims-free history</li>
            <li>Paperless billing or automatic payments with some insurers</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Build the right policy first. Then look for every legitimate way to reduce the cost without
            sacrificing the protection you intended to buy.
          </p>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              The best policy is not the one with the largest discount. It is the one that balances
              suitable protection with every savings opportunity you legitimately qualify for.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part V</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Deductibles change both premium and out-of-pocket responsibility.</li>
              <li>Percentage deductibles are often calculated from Dwelling Coverage.</li>
              <li>Rates can rise because of broader claim and rebuilding costs.</li>
              <li>Discounts should improve value without quietly dismantling protection.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-6">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part VI</p>
          <h2>Owning Your Policy</h2>
          <blockquote>
            Buying insurance is not the finish line. A good policy should grow and change as your life does.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-16" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 16</p>
          <h2>What Happens After You Say Yes</h2>

          <p>
            Buying a policy can feel like the end of the process. In reality, it is the beginning of
            owning one of the most important financial tools connected to your home.
          </p>

          <h3>Your mortgage company and escrow</h3>

          <p>
            If the home is financed, the mortgage company will usually require proof of insurance.
            Many homeowners pay through escrow, where part of the monthly mortgage payment is set aside
            and used to pay the renewal premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Escrow is convenient, but homeowners should still review renewals and confirm that updated
            insurance information reaches the mortgage company.
          </p>

          <h3>Your declarations page</h3>

          <p>
            The declarations page is the policy summary. It commonly shows the insured property,
            policy period, coverage limits, deductibles, endorsements, and premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you have a question, this is usually the first page worth reviewing.
          </p>

          <h3>Create a home inventory</h3>

          <p>
            Walk through the home with your phone. Record every room, closet, cabinet, garage,
            attic, and storage area. Save receipts, appraisals, and photographs for valuable items.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple twenty-minute video today may save countless hours after a major loss.
          </p>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Understand your declarations page, confirm escrow details, and keep a basic home inventory.
              These simple habits can make a difficult day much easier.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-17" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 17</p>
          <h2>When Your Life Changes, Your Policy Should Too</h2>

          <p>
            A remodeled kitchen, finished basement, new roof, detached workshop, solar panels,
            swimming pool, home-based business, or expensive piece of jewelry may change your insurance needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some changes increase needed coverage. Others qualify for discounts. Some introduce risks
            you may not have considered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not wait until after a claim to discover that the policy no longer reflects the home.
            A short annual review can keep it aligned with your life.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-18" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 18</p>
          <h2>When Should You Shop for Insurance?</h2>

          <p>
            Shopping is not about chasing the lowest number every year. It is about making sure you still
            receive strong value.
          </p>

          <p>It may be reasonable to compare options when:</p>

          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Your premium increases significantly</li>
            <li>You make major improvements to the home</li>
            <li>Your bundling situation changes</li>
            <li>Your current insurer no longer fits your needs</li>
            <li>You are consistently unhappy with service or communication</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Compare the coverages, deductibles, settlement methods, endorsements, exclusions, and service—not only the premium.
          </p>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Shop when there is a reason, and compare the policy before comparing the price.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <div class="part-summary">
            <p class="card-label">Key lessons from Part VI</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Escrow does not remove the homeowner's responsibility to review the policy.</li>
              <li>The declarations page is the best starting point for understanding current coverage.</li>
              <li>A home inventory can dramatically improve claim preparation.</li>
              <li>Policies should be reviewed when the home, family, or finances change.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="part-divider" id="part-7">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="part-number">Part VII</p>
          <h2>A Better Way to Think About Insurance</h2>
          <blockquote>
            Insurance is not about expecting the worst. It is about preparing wisely so the worst
            does not define your future.
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-19" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 19</p>
          <h2>The Biggest Mistakes Homeowners Make</h2>

          <h3>Waiting until after a loss to read the policy</h3>
          <p>
            The worst time to discover a misunderstanding is after the event has already happened.
            Read the declarations page and ask questions before you need the answers.
          </p>

          <h3>Assuming every insurance company is the same</h3>
          <p>
            Companies differ in underwriting, endorsements, discounts, policy options, and service.
          </p>

          <h3>Focusing only on price</h3>
          <p>
            Saving a few dollars each month can become expensive if the policy does not respond the way
            you expected after a loss.
          </p>

          <h3>Forgetting that life changes</h3>
          <p>
            Additions, renovations, new structures, valuables, family changes, and financial changes
            should trigger a policy review.
          </p>

          <aside class="takeaway-card">
            <p class="card-label">River Guide takeaway</p>
            <p>
              Most insurance mistakes happen long before a claim. Many can be prevented by understanding
              the policy and reviewing it regularly.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-20" class="chapter">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="chapter-number">Chapter 20</p>
          <h2>Questions Every Homeowner Should Ask</h2>

          <div class="question-grid">
            <section>
              <h3>About the home</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>How was my Dwelling Coverage calculated?</li>
                <li>Is my home insured based on estimated rebuilding cost?</li>
                <li>Does my policy include Building Codes Coverage?</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h3>About my belongings</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Is my personal property Replacement Cost or Actual Cash Value?</li>
                <li>Do I have enough Personal Property Coverage?</li>
                <li>Are there special limits for jewelry, firearms, art, instruments, or collectibles?</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h3>About liability</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Is $300,000 appropriate for my situation?</li>
                <li>Do my assets justify a higher limit or umbrella policy?</li>
                <li>How much Guest Medical Coverage do I have?</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h3>About deductibles and price</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Could I afford my deductible tomorrow?</li>
                <li>Is my wind and hail deductible a dollar amount or percentage?</li>
                <li>Which discounts am I receiving?</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="final-thought" class="final-reflection">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="eyebrow">One last thought</p>
          <h2>Understanding is part of protection.</h2>

          <p>
            Insurance is not about living in fear or expecting something bad to happen.
            It is about making one thoughtful decision today that can protect years of work tomorrow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of us hope we never file a homeowners claim. I hope you never have to.
            But if that day comes, I hope you are able to focus on taking care of your family
            rather than wondering whether you chose the right coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why this handbook exists—not to persuade you toward one company,
            but to help you understand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed homeowners make better decisions. Better decisions create stronger families,
            stronger communities, and greater peace of mind.
          </p>

          <p>Take care of your home. Take care of the people inside it. Keep asking good questions.</p>

          <p class="signature">— River Young</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="home-inventory" class="resource-section">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Resource 1</p>
          <h2>Home Inventory Checklist</h2>

          <div class="checklist">
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Record a slow video walkthrough of every room.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Open closets, cabinets, drawers, and storage areas.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Record the garage, attic, shed, and detached structures.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Photograph serial numbers for electronics and tools.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Save receipts and appraisals for valuable items.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Store a backup outside the home or in secure cloud storage.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Update the inventory after major purchases.</label>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="annual-review" class="resource-section">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Resource 2</p>
          <h2>Annual Policy Review</h2>

          <div class="checklist">
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm the dwelling amount still reflects the home.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Review the wind, hail, and all-other-perils deductibles.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm Personal Property is ACV or RCV as intended.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Review liability and Guest Medical limits.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Add renovations, detached structures, pools, solar, or new roofs.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Review jewelry, firearms, art, instruments, and collectibles.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm mortgage and escrow information is correct.</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Ask about new discounts or underwriting requirements.</label>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="glossary" class="resource-section">
        <div class="handbook-shell handbook-shell--reading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Resource 3</p>
          <h2>Plain-Language Glossary</h2>

          <dl class="glossary">
            <div>
              <dt>Actual Cash Value</dt>
              <dd>A valuation method that generally considers depreciation.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Additional Living Expense</dt>
              <dd>Coverage for certain increased living costs after a covered loss makes the home uninhabitable.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Deductible</dt>
              <dd>The amount the policyholder is responsible for before the insurer pays the remaining covered amount.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Declarations Page</dt>
              <dd>The policy summary showing major coverages, limits, deductibles, endorsements, dates, and premium.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Dwelling Coverage</dt>
              <dd>Coverage for the main structure and permanently attached parts of the home.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Endorsement</dt>
              <dd>A policy change that adds, removes, or modifies coverage.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Liability Coverage</dt>
              <dd>Coverage that may respond when the insured is legally responsible for covered injury or property damage to others.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Loss of Use</dt>
              <dd>Coverage that may help with additional living expenses after a covered loss.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Other Structures</dt>
              <dd>Coverage for qualifying detached structures on the insured property.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Replacement Cost</dt>
              <dd>A valuation method generally based on the cost to replace damaged property with comparable new property.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Scheduled Personal Property</dt>
              <dd>Additional coverage that individually lists certain valuable items.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </section>

